{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "והוריתיך", :translit "v'horiticha", :gloss "and I will teach you"} {:he "ה'", :translit "heh", :gloss "[the letter] heh"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Tagin (crown-like decorations) are drawn on the letter heh of והוריתיך, signaling an expanded meaning."}], :frame {:text "The verb והוריתיך appears in Exodus 4:12, where God tells Moses He will teach him what to speak. Ba'al HaTurim reads the tagin on the heh as a visual signal that the teaching God promises extends beyond the immediate context of the Exodus narrative to encompass the entirety of the Torah — all Five Books. The scribal practice of adorning certain letters with tagin is attested in sources such as Shabbat 89a and Menachot 29b, where special markings on letters carry interpretive significance.", :sources ["Exodus 4:12" "Shabbat 89a" "Menachot 29b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Tagin (crown-like decorations) are drawn on the letter heh of והוריתיך, signaling an expanded meaning.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:13:29.788832626Z", :model "glm-5.2"}